<BaseLayout
  title="Water Intake Calculator — Daily Hydration Needs — Nawtch"
  description="Estimate your daily water intake from body weight, exercise, and climate, with real IOM, EFSA, and ACSM sources cited."
  canonicalPath="/calculators/water/"
  bodyClass="calculators"
>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Water Intake Calculator</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Estimate your daily water needs from body weight, exercise, and climate.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/calculators/">← Back to Calculators</a>
  </header>

  <div id="calc-water"></div>

  <div id="calc-water-explain" class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How We Calculate This</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        The baseline figure here uses <strong>30–35 mL of water per kg of body weight per day</strong>, a common
        clinical rule of thumb (we use the 33 mL/kg midpoint) rather than a figure drawn directly from a single
        authoritative study. It's a practical starting point, not a precise requirement — actual needs vary by
        person, diet, and activity.
        <br /><br />
        For a more authoritative benchmark, the U.S. National Academies of Medicine (formerly the Institute of
        Medicine) set Adequate Intake levels for total water — from all beverages and food combined — at about <strong>3.7 L/day for men and 2.7 L/day for women</strong>. EFSA, the European Food Safety Authority, set
        its own Adequate Intakes at about <strong>2.5 L/day for men and 2.0 L/day for women</strong> under
        moderate temperature and activity conditions. Both bodies agree that people who exercise heavily or live
        in hot climates need more than these baseline figures.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Exercise adjustment:</strong> we add fluid using the American College of Sports Medicine's
        position stand on exercise and fluid replacement, which recommends replacing losses at a rate of roughly <strong>600–1200 mL per hour</strong> of exercise; we use a conservative 700 mL/hour estimate. Sweat rates
        vary a great deal between individuals and conditions, so treat this as a reasonable estimate rather than
        an exact figure — heavy or salty sweaters may need more, and pairing this with electrolytes matters for
        exercise over about an hour. Read more on that in our <a href="/supplements/electrolytes/">electrolytes page</a>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Climate adjustment:</strong> selecting "Hot / Humid" adds a flat 500 mL/day, a practical estimate
        reflecting the higher sweat losses of hot conditions — the National Academies' own guidance explicitly
        notes that people in hot climates may need more than the baseline Adequate Intake, though it does not
        specify an exact additional figure.
      </p>
      <a href="https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/10925/chapter/6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the National Academies' Dietary Reference Intakes for Water report →</a>
